{"concepts_used":["GC-0044","GC-0004","GC-0011","GC-0006"],"decline_reason":"","declined":false,"citations":[{"ref":"TMM/Part26/2#1","quote":"A ground for rejection should only be raised if the examiner is satisfied on the balance of probabilities that a ground exists. If the examiner is not so satisfied, a ground for rejection should not be raised."},{"ref":"TMM/Part26/2#1","quote":"(iii) In considering whether there is a likelihood of deception or confusion all surrounding circumstances have to be taken into consideration."}],"answer":"At examination, the examiner assesses whether a ground for rejection exists. The Manual says the examiner must be satisfied on the balance of probabilities before raising that ground (TMM/Part26/2#1).

